Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde (NES)
Enemy spawn logic
(Note this is mostly when playing as Jekyll but plenty of overlap with Hyde too.)

TLDR: There are three spawn systems. The first depends on what part of a stage you’re scrolling into, the second is more like a lottery, and the third is an extra ‘bomb man’ specific spawner.

The list. Every stage has a list, one entry for every 32 pixels of scrolling. Each entry is a type of enemy. As the screen scrolls, it reads the next entry and spawns that enemy if it can. The list is fixed, so these spawns are guaranteed provided a set of checks all pass.


The lottery. Once every 4-5 frames the game rolls a pair of dice to spawn an enemy if a set of checks all pass. Player input changes the dice – not directly, but by giving the game more or less work to do, which can change when the dice “roll” (and why I think it’s probably futile to try and engineer a non-TAS setup).


The bomb. Like the lottery, it runs once every 4-5 frames and keeps its own counter. Once it reaches a value (~every 14 seconds), a bomb man is spawned if you’re in a part of a stage where he can spawn (and all the other checks pass). In the final stage, the counter is ignored, so he can just respawn immediately. Note: bomb man can also spawn via the list system, so this 3rd system seems to be a way to ensure that he (and only he) will spawn repeatedly within certain areas.

Read on for a more in-depth explanation…
SYSTEM 1 - THE LIST
I like to think of it as a filmstrip attached to the stage moving along with it. A new frame of film every 32 pixels, and in that frame is a single enemy. So, e.g.,: …..nothing, nothing, cat, nothing, dog, bee, nothing…..

There’s a different filmstrip for each stage and mode (Jekyll vs. Hyde). More accurately, each film frame is more like a note about the enemy, specifying “what”, not “where.” Each enemy’s code itself determines left vs right spawn, for example.

Importantly, scrolling into a new film frame doesn’t guarantee a spawn, it just means the spawn check triggers (so 8 possible triggers per screen-width). Multiple additional checks must pass for the spawn to happen. These include things like: is the enemy not already alive? Is there room for them? So, for example, if a film frame triggers and it says “dog”, but there’s already a dog spawned, then no new spawn happens. (And most enemies have a max of 1, but there’s exceptions like spiders (2) and bees (4).)

In the US ROM, there are 3 list entries whose spawns have been disabled. One is the women who throw things at each other from the windows. Not sure what the other two are.
SYSTEM 2 - THE LOTTERY
You can think of part of the game’s workload as sections on a racetrack. Each lap takes 4-5 frames. The lottery spawn system is one job on the track, so it gets one turn every 4-5 frames. (Player movement is another job on the track, which is why the controls feel clunky; and let’s not even go near the madness of the stage 6 ‘speedup’ – DisplacedGamers already explained that beautifully).

When the spawn manager gets its turn, it asks four questions. Any answer of “no” ends the turn immediately with nothing spawned.
Is this stage one that spawns anything via this system? Only stages 1, 3, and 4 use the lottery system.
Is Jekyll in the right part of the level? Each stage allows spawns only in specific sections. (In fact, for most of the game the lottery system isn’t used.)
Did the dice roll appropriately? A bit complicated, but one reduces to a coin-flip, so about half of all turns spawn nothing. A second, stricter test on the same counter passes about a quarter of the time. When both pass, the 2nd dice’s roll is used to select 1 of 3 enemies. The values are not equally likely –  the man and woman are about twice as likely compared to Billy. (Side note: the dice also determine what the enemy looks like, via sprite set and palette.)
Is there room? Even if the first 3 all passed, the spawn is still going to get locked out here a lot of the time. Each spawned enemy has an “alive” flag, and while it’s set, that enemy can’t spawn again (a few enemies have a different “# alive” cap, as discussed). Enemies also share sprite slots, and those need to be free too. Some are mutually exclusive. You can’t have both a lottery-spawned Billy and a lottery-spawned man, for example. There’s cross-checking with system 1’s spawns too, eliminating even more possibilities.

When all 4 questions answer “yes”, an enemy spawns. At 4-5 frames per turn, that’s ~13-14 spawn attempts per second – and the majority of those will fail.

One interesting extra bit of info: When the bomb man drops his bomb, it temporarily switches off the lottery spawn system (and makes the townspeople fast & dangerous). It turns back on after about 3.5 seconds.
SYSTEM 3 - THE BOMB
On the same track as the lottery, so it gets a turn every 4-5 frames. It increments a counter that triggers a bomb man spawn check every ~14 seconds. The check runs through some questions: Is the bomb man already alive? Is there room for him? Is Jekyll in a particular section of the stage? If the answer is yes to all, a bomb man is spawned.

Note that this system is a recurring timer, not a lottery or a single scroll trigger. Stand still in a part of a stage where bomb man can spawn (via this system), and he’ll spawn infinitely every ~14 seconds (but only 1 at a time, of course).

Note also that stage six drops the timer, so the spawn attempt runs every lap – that’s why in 3 certain sections of stage six he comes back as soon as he leaves.
THE DICE AREN’T REALLY DICE
The two memory values we’ve been calling dice are updated by a small routine that runs every frame (during NMI). But it’s not really a random-number generator. During the routine, the game fetches a number and adds it to dice #1, then fetches another number and adds it to dice #2. But it gets these numbers from its own program code (interpreted as numbers), and because the game uses bank switching, the numbers that come back give the impression of randomness – even though they are deterministic. (If you waited ~18 minutes, they’d loop, returning to their starting values.)

While it might be technically true that it’s not random, it’s unhelpful for normal play because of the previously mentioned input phase perturbation (sorry, I know that sounds horrible, but all I mean is that input throws off the ‘cycle’ or ‘when the dice roll’).

It does mean you could calculate the entire sequence for TAS manipulation, if you really wanted to (no thanks).
HOW INPUT CHANGES THE LOTTERY
Put simply, input can change how much work a frame does, which can increase or decrease the likelihood that the dice updating routine runs. That’s because the NMI has an alternative path it can take that skips a whole bunch of work if it happens to run when the game is in the middle of certain other work – and surprise surprise, some of the work it skips is the dice update routine. That’s why input causes the dice “phase” to shift.

Put another way: When the game is “busy”, it doesn’t update the dice, and player input can shift when a “busy” frame lands.
